  "seven_q": {
    "Q0_posture": "This document takes a critical, destructive posture before inquiry, starting with what can kill an idea rather than what supports it.",
    "Q1_identity": "This is a methodological framework for testing claims through destruction rather than confirmation, presented as a reverse of the traditional scientific method.",
    "Q2_domain": "It lives primarily in epistemology and philosophy of science, with applications to physics and theology.",
    "Q3_claim": "The claim is that identity should be earned through survival of destruction testing, not assumed at the start, and that this reverse method avoids confirmation bias.",
    "Q4_support": "It is grounded in logical necessity (contradiction, infinite regress), empirical testability, and the structure of the 7Q framework itself.",
    "Q5_dependencies": "It depends on the existence of kill conditions, the possibility of survival, and the axioms of distinction and existence.",
    "Q6_consequences": "It forces that any claim must be testable against destruction, that identity is earned, and that evidence confirms rather than constructs.",
    "Q7_kill": "It dies if no kill conditions can be defined, if survival is impossible, if the method is circular, or if it fails to produce consequences."
  },
